---
// Owner's guide shown only on the Santa Monica submarket page: how Santa Monica's own rules (rent
// control, the yearly adjustment, transfer taxes, earthquake retrofits, SB 79) differ from the City of
// Los Angeles. The Measure GS section (MeasureGsCliff) is passed in through the "gs" slot so it sits
// between the rent control and transfer tax parts. Sources, checked October 22, 2026:
// - Rent control: City of Santa Monica rent control page (santamonica.gov/rent-control-charter-amendment-regulations).
//   Charter Amendment adopted by voters in April 1979. General Adjustment by Rent Control Board resolution:
//   2.6% with a $70 limit from Sept 1, 2026 (Res. 26-001), 2.3% with $60 from Sept 1, 2025 (Res. 25-001), 3%
//   with $76 from Sept 1, 2024 (Res. 24-002). Chapter 12 exempts owner-occupied properties of three units or
//   fewer, hotels and motels; Chapters 11 and 13 cover annual registration fees and landlord registration.
//   "Buildings built before 1979" and Costa-Hawkins vacancy resets since 1999 are from Santa Monica Daily
//   Press and Santa Monica Next coverage, so the page tells owners to confirm their building with the city.
// - Transfer taxes: LA County Registrar-Recorder list of city rates, the same figures used by MeasureGsCliff
//   ($3, $6, and $56 per $1,000 at under $5M, $5M to $7.99M, and $8M and up, each on the whole price) and by
//   the West Hollywood guide for the City of LA (ULA 4% above $5.4M and 5.5% at $10.9M, $4.50 per $1,000 city
//   tax, $1.10 per $1,000 county tax).
// - Seismic: City of Santa Monica Seismic Retrofit Program page. Ordinance adopted March 2017, over 2,000
//   buildings identified, mostly soft-story with tuck-under parking, "Order to Structurally Analyze" notices.
//   The page does not state deadlines, so none are given here.
// - SB 79: City of Santa Monica SB 79 page. Four Metro E Line stops (Downtown Santa Monica, 17th Street/Santa
//   Monica College, Bergamot Station, Expo/Bundy); effective July 1, 2026; Exclusion Ordinance adopted July 28,
//   2026, effective August 27, 2026, excluding six of seven transit zones until 2030; Expo/Bundy was being
//   handled through separate zoning changes, so its status is left for owners to confirm.
// Sales figures come from SALES_STATS, so they refresh with that file.
import PricePerUnitChart from './PricePerUnitChart.astro';
import { SALES_STATS, VALUATION_DATA_PERIOD } from '../data/valuationAssumptions';
import { closings } from '../data/closings';

const money = (n: number) => `$${Math.round(n).toLocaleString('en-US')}`;

// ---- Transfer tax on the same building in the City of LA and in Santa Monica.
const laTax = (price: number) => {
	const county = (price / 1000) * 1.1;
	const city = (price / 1000) * 4.5;
	const ula = price > 5_400_000 ? price * (price >= 10_900_000 ? 0.055 : 0.04) : 0;
	return { county, city, ula, total: county + city + ula };
};
const smTax = (price: number) => {
	const county = (price / 1000) * 1.1;
	const city = price * (price >= 8_000_000 ? 0.056 : price >= 5_000_000 ? 0.006 : 0.003);
	return { county, city, ula: 0, total: county + city };
};
const scenarios = [6_000_000, 9_000_000].map((price) => {
	const la = laTax(price);
	const sm = smTax(price);
	return { price, la, sm, max: Math.max(la.total, sm.total), diff: sm.total - la.total };
});

// ---- Yearly allowed increase (General Adjustment), with its dollar limit.
const gaHistory = [
	{ label: 'Sept 2024', pct: 3, limit: 76 },
	{ label: 'Sept 2025', pct: 2.3, limit: 60 },
	{ label: 'Sept 2026', pct: 2.6, limit: 70 },
];
const CURRENT = gaHistory[gaHistory.length - 1];
const BAR_MAX = 3.5; // the tallest bar fills this share of the chart
const BAR_SCALE = 78; // percent of the chart height

// ---- "Take your increase before you list": what an unclaimed increase is worth at Santa Monica cap rates.
const smStats = SALES_STATS['santa-monica'].rso;
const midCap = smStats.capRate ? (smStats.capRate.low + smStats.capRate.high) / 2 : 0.054;
const EX_UNITS = 10;
const EX_RENT = 2000;
const extraYearly = EX_UNITS * EX_RENT * (CURRENT.pct / 100) * 12;
const extraValue = Math.round(extraYearly / midCap / 1000) * 1000;

const soldHere = closings.filter((c) => c.submarket === 'santa-monica');
---

<section class="section smg">
	<div class="container smg__narrow">
		<span class="eyebrow">Its Own City, Its Own Rules</span>
		<h2>Selling an Apartment Building in Santa Monica vs. the City of Los Angeles</h2>
		<p>
			Santa Monica is not part of the City of Los Angeles, and that matters when you sell. Its rent control law, its yearly rent increase, its
			transfer tax, its earthquake retrofit program, and its answer to the new state transit-housing law are all Santa Monica&rsquo;s own. The
			same building can face very different costs and rules a few blocks across the city line.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="container">
		<figure class="smg-compare" aria-labelledby="smg-compare-cap">
			<figcaption id="smg-compare-cap" class="smg-figcap">Same kind of building, two sets of rules</figcaption>
			<div class="smg-compare__grid" role="table" aria-describedby="smg-compare-cap">
				<div class="smg-compare__row smg-compare__row--head" role="row">
					<span role="columnheader"></span>
					<span role="columnheader">City of Los Angeles</span>
					<span role="columnheader" class="smg-compare__sm">Santa Monica</span>
				</div>
				<div class="smg-compare__row" role="row">
					<span role="rowheader">Rent control covers</span>
					<span role="cell">Buildings first occupied on or before Oct 1, 1978</span>
					<span role="cell" class="smg-compare__sm">Buildings built before 1979 (Charter Amendment adopted April 1979)</span>
				</div>
				<div class="smg-compare__row" role="row">
					<span role="rowheader">Yearly rent increase</span>
					<span role="cell">3% from July 1, 2026, set by the LA Housing Department</span>
					<span role="cell" class="smg-compare__sm">{CURRENT.pct}% from Sept 1, 2026, with a ${CURRENT.limit} limit, set by the Rent Control Board</span>
				</div>
				<div class="smg-compare__row" role="row">
					<span role="rowheader">Transfer tax on large sales</span>
					<span role="cell">Measure ULA: 4% of the full price above $5.4M, 5.5% at $10.9M and up</span>
					<span role="cell" class="smg-compare__sm">Measure GS: 5.6% of the full price at $8M and up</span>
				</div>
				<div class="smg-compare__row" role="row">
					<span role="rowheader">Earthquake retrofit</span>
					<span role="cell">LA&rsquo;s mandatory soft-story program</span>
					<span role="cell" class="smg-compare__sm">Seismic Retrofit Ordinance (March 2017)</span>
				</div>
				<div class="smg-compare__row" role="row">
					<span role="rowheader">SB 79 transit housing law</span>
					<span role="cell">Phased in, with full implementation delayed to 2030 in historic districts and some other areas</span>
					<span role="cell" class="smg-compare__sm">Six of seven transit zones excluded until 2030</span>
				</div>
			</div>
		</figure>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section section-alt smg">
	<div class="container smg__narrow">
		<span class="eyebrow">Santa Monica Rent Control</span>
		<h2>Santa Monica Rent Control, in Plain English</h2>
		<p>
			Santa Monica voters passed rent control in April 1979, and the city runs it through an elected Rent Control Board. It is separate from LA&rsquo;s
			ordinance, and it has its own yearly increase, its own registration, and its own exemptions. Here is how to tell where your building stands.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="container">
		<figure class="smg-flow" aria-labelledby="smg-flow-cap">
			<figcaption id="smg-flow-cap" class="smg-figcap">Is my Santa Monica building rent-controlled?</figcaption>
			<div class="smg-flow__q">Was the building <strong>built before 1979</strong>?</div>
			<div class="smg-flow__branches">
				<div class="smg-flow__branch">
					<span class="smg-flow__answer">Yes</span>
					<div class="smg-flow__box smg-flow__box--yes">
						<strong>Covered by Santa Monica rent control</strong>
						<ul>
							<li>Rent can rise by the General Adjustment the Rent Control Board sets each year ({CURRENT.pct}% from Sept 1, 2026)</li>
							<li>Each controlled unit has to be registered with the Rent Control Board</li>
							<li>Registration fees are due every year</li>
							<li>Owner-occupied properties of three units or fewer are exempt</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="smg-flow__branch">
					<span class="smg-flow__answer smg-flow__answer--no">No</span>
					<div class="smg-flow__box">
						<strong>No city rent cap, but state rules may apply</strong>
						<ul>
							<li>Once a building is more than 15 years old, the statewide AB 1482 cap may apply</li>
							<li>Confirm the status of your building with the Rent Control Board or an attorney before you price or sell</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</figure>
	</div>

	<div class="container smg__narrow">
		<h3>How much can rent go up each year?</h3>
		<p>
			Each September, the Rent Control Board sets the General Adjustment by resolution, and it comes with a dollar limit. For 2026 the adjustment is
			<strong>{CURRENT.pct}%, with a ${CURRENT.limit} limit</strong> on the dollar increase. That is much tighter than the statewide AB 1482 cap (5% plus
			inflation, up to 10%), which is why Santa Monica buyers pay close attention to how far below market the current rents are.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="container">
		<figure class="smg-bars" aria-labelledby="smg-bars-cap">
			<figcaption id="smg-bars-cap" class="smg-figcap">Allowed yearly rent increase in Santa Monica</figcaption>
			<div class="smg-bars__chart">
				{
					gaHistory.map((y, i) => (
						<div class="smg-bars__col">
							<span class={`smg-bars__bar${i === gaHistory.length - 1 ? ' smg-bars__bar--now' : ''}`} style={`height: ${(y.pct / BAR_MAX) * BAR_SCALE}%`}>
								<span class="smg-bars__val">{y.pct}%</span>
							</span>
							<span class="smg-bars__label">
								{y.label}
								<br />
								${y.limit} limit
							</span>
						</div>
					))
				}
			</div>
			<p class="smg-source">Each figure applies from September 1 through the following August 31. Source: City of Santa Monica Rent Control Board resolutions 24-002, 25-001, and 26-001.</p>
		</figure>
	</div>

	<div class="container smg__narrow">
		<h3>Four rent control rules every Santa Monica seller should know</h3>
		<ol class="smg-rules">
			<li>
				<strong>Market rent comes back only when a tenant leaves on their own.</strong> Under the state&rsquo;s Costa-Hawkins law, an owner can set a new rent when
				a tenant moves out voluntarily, and Santa Monica has allowed this since 1999. Once a new tenant moves in, that rent is controlled again.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Every controlled unit has to be registered.</strong> The city&rsquo;s regulations cover landlord registration and re-registration of rent
				levels, plus annual registration fees. Buyers and their attorneys will ask for yours.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>The yearly increase is set by the Board, and it changes.</strong> It was 3% in 2024, 2.3% in 2025, and 2.6% in 2026, each with its own
				dollar limit. Use the current figure when you price, not the one from last year.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Small owner-occupied buildings and newer buildings are treated differently.</strong> The city exempts owner-occupied properties of three
				units or fewer. Buildings built in 1979 or later are outside the rent caps, though the statewide AB 1482 cap may apply.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p class="smg-source">Confirm how these rules apply to your building with the Santa Monica Rent Control Board or a landlord-tenant attorney.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<slot name="gs" />

<section class="section smg">
	<div class="container smg__narrow">
		<span class="eyebrow">Transfer Taxes</span>
		<h2>Santa Monica vs. the City of Los Angeles: Which Costs More to Sell?</h2>
		<p>
			It depends on the price. Below $8 million, Santa Monica&rsquo;s transfer tax is far lower than the City of LA&rsquo;s, because LA&rsquo;s Measure ULA
			starts at $5.4 million. At $8 million and up, Santa Monica&rsquo;s Measure GS rate of 5.6% is higher than LA&rsquo;s ULA rate of 4%. Here is the same
			price, either side of the line.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="container">
		{
			scenarios.map((sc, idx) => (
				<figure class="smg-tax" aria-labelledby={`smg-tax-cap-${idx}`}>
					<figcaption id={`smg-tax-cap-${idx}`} class="smg-figcap">
						Transfer taxes on a {money(sc.price)} apartment building
					</figcaption>
					<div class="smg-tax__row">
						<span class="smg-tax__name">City of Los Angeles</span>
						<span class="smg-tax__track">
							{sc.la.ula > 0 && <span class="smg-tax__seg smg-tax__seg--ula" style={`width: ${(sc.la.ula / sc.max) * 100}%`} title="Measure ULA" />}
							<span class="smg-tax__seg smg-tax__seg--city" style={`width: ${(sc.la.city / sc.max) * 100}%`} title="LA city transfer tax" />
							<span class="smg-tax__seg smg-tax__seg--county" style={`width: ${(sc.la.county / sc.max) * 100}%`} title="LA County transfer tax" />
						</span>
						<span class={`smg-tax__amt${sc.la.total < sc.sm.total ? ' smg-tax__amt--good' : ''}`}>{money(sc.la.total)}</span>
					</div>
					<div class="smg-tax__row">
						<span class="smg-tax__name">Santa Monica</span>
						<span class="smg-tax__track">
							<span class="smg-tax__seg smg-tax__seg--city" style={`width: ${(sc.sm.city / sc.max) * 100}%`} title="Santa Monica city transfer tax" />
							<span class="smg-tax__seg smg-tax__seg--county" style={`width: ${(sc.sm.county / sc.max) * 100}%`} title="LA County transfer tax" />
						</span>
						<span class={`smg-tax__amt${sc.sm.total < sc.la.total ? ' smg-tax__amt--good' : ''}`}>{money(sc.sm.total)}</span>
					</div>
					<p class={`smg-tax__callout${sc.diff > 0 ? ' smg-tax__callout--cost' : ''}`}>
						{sc.diff < 0 ? (
							<>
								Difference: <strong>{money(-sc.diff)}</strong> less in transfer taxes on the Santa Monica side.
							</>
						) : (
							<>
								Difference: <strong>{money(sc.diff)}</strong> more in transfer taxes on the Santa Monica side.
							</>
						)}
					</p>
				</figure>
			))
		}
		<ul class="smg-tax__legend">
			<li><span class="smg-tax__key smg-tax__seg--ula" />Measure ULA (City of LA only)</li>
			<li><span class="smg-tax__key smg-tax__seg--city" />City transfer tax (LA or Santa Monica, including Measure GS)</li>
			<li><span class="smg-tax__key smg-tax__seg--county" />LA County transfer tax ($1.10 per $1,000)</li>
		</ul>
		<p class="smg-source">
			Illustration only, before commissions and other closing costs. Rates from the LA County Registrar-Recorder and the City of Los Angeles Office of
			Finance (ULA thresholds for closings after June 30, 2026). Talk to your tax advisor about your own sale.
		</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section section-alt smg">
	<div class="container smg__narrow">
		<span class="eyebrow">Fresh Advice for Owners</span>
		<h2>Three Moves to Make Before You List a Santa Monica Building</h2>
	</div>

	<div class="container smg-moves">
		<div class="smg-move">
			<span class="smg-move__num">1</span>
			<h3>Take this year&rsquo;s rent increase first</h3>
			<p>
				Buyers pay for the rent roll they see, not the one you could have had. If you have not taken the {CURRENT.pct}% General Adjustment, check the Rent
				Control Board&rsquo;s notice rules and serve it before you list.
			</p>
			<div class="smg-math" aria-label={`Example: ${EX_UNITS} units at ${money(EX_RENT)} a month`}>
				<div class="smg-math__line"><span>{EX_UNITS} units &times; {money(EX_RENT)} rent &times; {CURRENT.pct}%</span><span>{money(extraYearly / 12)} / month</span></div>
				<div class="smg-math__line"><span>Over a year</span><span>{money(extraYearly)}</span></div>
				<div class="smg-math__line smg-math__line--total">
					<span>Added value at a {(midCap * 100).toFixed(1)}% cap rate</span><span>about {money(extraValue)}</span>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div class="smg-move">
			<span class="smg-move__num">2</span>
			<h3>Look up your building in the seismic program</h3>
			<p>
				Santa Monica&rsquo;s Seismic Retrofit Ordinance, adopted in March 2017, identified over 2,000 commercial and multifamily buildings as potentially
				vulnerable, most of them soft-story buildings with tuck-under parking. Owners on the list get an order to have the building analyzed by a licensed
				professional, and a building found non-compliant must be retrofitted on the city&rsquo;s timeline. Being on the list is not a finding that a
				building is unsafe. Check the city&rsquo;s searchable map or compliance schedule, and gather the engineer&rsquo;s report, plans, and permits so a
				buyer does not have to ask.
			</p>
		</div>
		<div class="smg-move">
			<span class="smg-move__num">3</span>
			<h3>Check whether SB 79 applies to your lot</h3>
			<p>
				SB 79, the state transit-housing law, took effect July 1, 2026 and sets minimum building standards within a half mile of transit stops. Four
				Metro E Line stops count in Santa Monica: Downtown Santa Monica, 17th Street/Santa Monica College, Bergamot Station, and Expo/Bundy. The city adopted
				an ordinance, effective August 27, 2026, that excludes six of its seven transit zones until 2030, and sites with designated historic resources are
				excluded too. The city&rsquo;s own map is approximate, so confirm your parcel with the city and ask us to price it both ways.
			</p>
			<p class="smg-move__proof">
				Santa Monica is a repeat market for us: we have closed {soldHere.length} sales here, including
				<a href="/closed-sales/829-euclid-st-santa-monica-ca-90403/">829 Euclid St</a>.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section smg">
	<div class="container smg__narrow">
		<span class="eyebrow">Prices Across LA</span>
		<h2>What Santa Monica Apartment Buildings Sell For</h2>
		<p>
			Santa Monica&rsquo;s older, rent-controlled buildings sold for a median of {money(smStats.pricePerUnit!.median)} per unit in {VALUATION_DATA_PERIOD}, across
			{smStats.pricePerUnit!.compCount} sales{
				smStats.capRate ? `, with cap rates typically between ${(smStats.capRate.low * 100).toFixed(2)}% and ${(smStats.capRate.high * 100).toFixed(2)}%` : ''
			}. Here is how that compares with the other neighborhoods we cover.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="container">
		<PricePerUnitChart highlight="santa-monica" />
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section section-alt smg">
	<div class="container smg__narrow">
		<span class="eyebrow">Seller Checklist</span>
		<h2>What to Gather Before Selling a Santa Monica Apartment Building</h2>
		<ul class="smg-check">
			<li>Current rent roll with each tenant&rsquo;s move-in date and current rent</li>
			<li>Proof the building&rsquo;s Rent Control Board registration and annual fees are current</li>
			<li>Copies of the last General Adjustment notices served</li>
			<li>Seismic program paperwork: the city&rsquo;s order, the engineer&rsquo;s analysis, plans, permits, and sign-off (if your building is on the city&rsquo;s list)</li>
			<li>Your SB 721 balcony and stair inspection report, if the building has elevated wood balconies, decks, or stairs (see our <a href="/insights/sb-721-balcony-inspection-los-angeles-apartment-owners/">SB 721 guide</a>)</li>
			<li>Your parcel&rsquo;s transit-zone status for SB 79 from the city</li>
			<li>Two years of operating expenses and utility bills</li>
			<li>Any buyout agreements, relocation payments, or pending tenant matters</li>
			<li>Leases, including any pet, parking, or storage agreements</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Not sure where to start? We will walk you through it as part of a free Broker Opinion of Value. <a href="/contact/?intent=bov">Request yours here</a>.
		</p>
	</div>
</section>

<style>
